WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.740
Hey everyone, how is it going?

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.380
Today we're going to be talking about coding bootcamps,

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.040
And while we're on the topic of non traditional ways to get into software engineering

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.960
I want to say thank you to the Great Courses Plus for sponsoring today's video

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.060
Coding bootcamps are short and intensive programs to learn

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.060
Software engineering skills, with the ultimate goal of getting a job

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.020
They're a great accelerated way to get into software engineering

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.660
Without needing to go through a 4 year degree program at a university.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.420
They vary in duration but typically are around 12 weeks long

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.120
and demand about 40 to 60 hours a week of work.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.140
Coding bootcamps teach you all the essential things you need to know to become a software engineer.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.340
They teach you important computer science foundations and the real appeal is that

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.460
They also teach you highly specialized skills to become a specific type of software engineer

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.320
Think of it like a trade school if you will

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.960
For instance, if you're aiming to become a web engineer, a coding bootcamp will teach you

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.540
Ruby on Rails, React and Javascript so you can get a job as web engineer

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.080
Most coding bootcamp are held in person and you'll be part of

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.700
A cohort with a bunch of other people who are aspiring to be software engineers

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.320
There will be instructors giving classes on important software engineering topics.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:20.580
You'll add these projects to your portfolio which you can list at your resume and you can talk about in interviews.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.720
Most programs will also help you with the job prep process like helping to refine your resume

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.620
Or giving you resources on how to ace a job interview

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.680
I think this is awesome because a lot of times, no one is actually formally teaching you how to do these things,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.300
Even in college

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.880
And getting advice from people who really familiar with the

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.540
Job and interview process in the tech industry is really useful

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:45.895
Bootcamps can be highly selective so you usually go through

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.800
An application and interview process to get into the bootcamp that you want

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.115
Because the bootcamps are very rigorous and highly demanding

00:01:53.115 --> 00:01:56.335
They'll check to see whether you have what it takes to complete the course

00:01:56.515 --> 00:01:59.915
So hearing all this, you might be wondering, how much do they cost?

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.400
It depends from bootcamp to bootcamp and what city it's in

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.660
But some bootcamps take a percentage of your salary and other ones require an upfront cost

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:13.515
For instance, some bootcamps don't require payment until 3 months after you've gotten your first job

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.460
Meaning you don't have to pay anything until then, so it kind of works like a loan.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.565
Other bootcamps will require an upfront cost

00:02:20.565 --> 00:02:23.515
and they'll usually be at least a couple thousand US dollars

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.780
I think the largest concentration  bootcamps in the US are here in the Bay Area

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.280
Mostly notably ones like Hack Reactor, App Academy and Hackbright

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.160
I know they're popping up in other areas too, so if you know any in your area

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.260
Then post in the comments down below

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.040
I honestly think bootcamps are awesome

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.800
It enables people who didn't have a chance to study computer science previously

00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:47.815
Or maybe those who have found new passion for software engineering

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.340
To become a software engineer quickly.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.060
There are so many software engineering jobs available

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.080
And it's no secret that we need more people in tech.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.600
And bootcamps make this whole thing a lot easier for everyone involved.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.680
I think going to bootcamps is just as great a way to become a software engineer

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.880
as it is to have a degree in computer science.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.100
There are bootcamps targeted to get those from under represented minorities

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.000
into sustainable careers in technology,

00:03:12.005 --> 00:03:14.825
And I seriously think that's so cool!

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.620
One thing to note though, is that while bootcamps are a great fast track way to become a software engineer

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.100
It isn't a guaranteed or easy way to get a job

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:28.220
I've heard from friends who've been to bootcamps that the hardest thing about going to a bootcamp

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:31.700
Is not the bootcamp itself, but it's getting that first job.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:37.420
Everyone applies to hundreds of companies and it can takes months to find that first job.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.060
That part can be really difficult but I think that it gets easier after that first job

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.500
My friends all now have software engineering jobs,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.660
and they're really happy because they get to do what they love.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.880
I don't mean to be a downer about it, I think bootcamps are still a great way to get into software engineering

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.360
But I just wanted to let you know, regardless.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:59.080
If you've been to a bootcamp before then please leave a comment down below with your experiences

